╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ simplify_to_proven_gen1_structure                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The current butterfly-based ansatz achieves a combined score of 1.21, significantly       │
│                          │ underperforming both prior programs (1.40 and 1.44). Analysis reveals:                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Current Issues:**                                                                       │
│                          │ - Test accuracy drops to 0.99 (vs 1.00 in priors)                                         │
│                          │ - Validation/test loss worsen (0.14/0.12 vs 0.05/0.05)                                    │
│                          │ - Convergence slows (240 steps vs 150)                                                    │
│                          │ - Despite only 62 params, gate_count explodes to 396 (vs 228 in Gen1)                     │
│                          │ - Architecture complexity does not translate to better expressiveness                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Root Cause:**                                                                           │
│                          │ The butterfly multi-scale entanglement with 38 parametrized/fixed gates per block creates │
│                          │ excessive circuit depth and coupling complexity without sufficient parameter control. The │
│                          │ extreme parameter sharing (only 2 unique RY parameters across 4 qubits, etc.) limits      │
│                          │ adaptability during training.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
